For every fixed $k\ge1$ and every bounded continuous function $F:[0,1]^k\to\mathbb R$, $$\label{eq:main-law}
 \lim_{x\to\infty}\frac1{\pi(x)-1}
     \sum_{\substack{3\le p\le x\\p\text{ prime}}}
       F\bigl(V_1(p),\ldots,V_k(p)\bigr)
 =\mathbb EF(L_1,\ldots,L_k).$$ The limit holds through all real $x$ and uses ordinary equal weighting of the primes.

The prime number theorem holds with an error smaller than every fixed negative power of the logarithm. In particular, as $y\to\infty$, $$\sum_{p\le y}\frac1p=\log\log y+c_M+o(1),
 \qquad
 \prod_{p\le y}\left(1-\frac1p\right)
       \sim\frac{e^{-\gamma_E}}{\log y},$$ where $c_M$ and $\gamma_E$ are constants. For fixed $A,C>0$, uniformly for $r\le(\log y)^C$ and $(a,r)=1$, $$\#\{p\le y:p\equiv a\pmod r\}
 =\frac{\mathop{\mathrm{Li}}(y)}{\varphi(r)}
     +O_{A,C}\!\left(\frac{y}{(\log y)^A}\right).$$ The constants need not be effective.

For every fixed nonnegative integer $k$ there is $C_k$ such that $$\sum_{n\le Y}\tau(n)^k\ll_k Y(\log(2Y))^{C_k},\qquad
 \sum_{n\le Y}\frac{\tau(n)^k}{n}
       \ll_k(\log(2Y))^{C_k}.$$ Let $P(t)=\sum_{n\le Y}c_n n^{it}$. On every real interval $I$ of length $T\ge0$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \int_I|P(t)|^2\,\mathrm dt
 &\ll (T+Y\log(2Y))\sum_n|c_n|^2,\label{eq:dirichlet-mean}\\
 \sum_{t\in\mathcal T}|P(t)|^2
 &\ll (T+1+Y\log(2Y))(\log(2Y))^2
                  \sum_n|c_n|^2,\label{eq:dirichlet-spaced}
\end{aligned}$$ whenever $\mathcal T\subset I$ has mutual spacing at least one. The constants in these two inequalities are absolute and do not depend on how the coefficients were formed.

Fix $\delta>0$, $C>0$, $0<a<1$ and $A>0$. Suppose $x^\delta\le H\le x^C$ and $I\subset[H,2H]$ is an interval. Then $$\label{eq:rough-count}
 \#\{n\in I:P^-(n)>W\}=V(W)|I|+O(HL^{-A}).$$ If $d\le\exp(L^a)$ and every prime divisor of $d$ exceeds $W$, then for every residue $b\pmod d$, $$\label{eq:rough-off-sieve-progression}
 \#\{n\in I:n\equiv b\pmod d,\ P^-(n)>W\}
 =\frac{V(W)|I|}{d}+O\!\left(\frac Hd L^{-A}\right).$$ For every character $\chi$ modulo $q\le L^C$, $$\label{eq:rough-character}
 \sum_{\substack{n\in I\\P^-(n)>W}}\chi(n)
 =\mathbf{1}_{\chi\text{ principal}}V(W)|I|+O(HL^{-A}).$$ All statements are uniform in the indicated intervals and residues.

Consider finitely many objects with nonnegative weights and a bad condition at each of some designated primes $p\le z$. Suppose that the total weight on which all conditions indexed by the primes dividing a squarefree $d$ hold is $$Xg(d)+r(d),$$ including $d=1$, where $X\ge0$, $g$ is multiplicative, and fixed $\eta_0>0,C_0$ satisfy $$0\le g(p)\le1-\eta_0,
 \qquad \sum_{w<p\le w^2}g(p)\le C_0\quad(w>1).$$ For every sufficiently large even integer $h$, the weight avoiding all designated bad conditions is $$\label{eq:block-sieve}
 X\prod_{p\le z}(1-g(p))\bigl(1+O(e^{-h})\bigr)
 +O\!\left(\sum_{\substack{d\le z^{4h+2}\\d\text{ squarefree}}}
          |r(d)|\right).$$ Products and sums use only designated primes. Constants depend only on the density bounds and are uniform in $h$. For $h=2$, an upper bound holds with a fixed constant times the main term and the same remainder sum.

Fix $\delta,C,D_*>0$ and $q\in(0,1)$. Suppose $H_m,H_n\geq x^\delta$ and $X=H_mH_n\asymp x$. Let $F$ satisfy $|F(h)|\leq1$ and $F(ph)=F(h)$ for every prime in the groups <a href="#det:bands" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="det:bands">[det:bands]</a>. Let $\alpha_m$ be supported on an arbitrary interval in $[H_m,2H_m]$, where it has the value $$\alpha_m=m^{iv}\left(\mathbf{1}_{m\text{ prime}}
       -\frac{\mathbf{1}_{m\text{ rough}}}{V(W)\log m}\right),
 \qquad |v|\leq L^C.$$ Let $\beta_n$ be supported on rough integers in $[H_n,2H_n]$, with $|\beta_n|\leq L^C$. If $K$ is sufficiently large in terms of $\delta,C,D_*,q$, then $$\label{eq:type-II}
 \left|\sum_{m,n}\alpha_m\beta_n F(mn-1)\mathcal W(mn-1)\right|
       \ll X L^{-D_*}.$$ The required lower bound on $K$ is independent of the particular $a_i$. The implicit constant and the threshold for $x$ may depend on all the fixed parameters, including the $a_i$.

There is $c_\delta>0$ such that the following holds. Let $S$ be squarefree with $S\leq\exp(C_KL^{0.98})$, and prescribe $g_0\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z/S\mathbb Z)$. If $I_1,I_2,I_3$ are intervals in $[1,16],[1,2],[0,1]$, respectively, the number of primitive roots satisfying $$u/U\in I_1,\qquad v/V\in I_2,\qquad r\in I_3,
 \qquad g\equiv g_0\pmod S$$ is $$\label{det:root-residue-count}
 \frac{UV\,|I_1||I_2||I_3|}
      {\zeta(2)|\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z/S\mathbb Z)|}
             +O(UVx^{-c_\delta}).$$ The error is uniform in the intervals and in $g_0$.

For every fixed $E_0>0$, one can choose $A_0$ sufficiently large and then $K$ sufficiently large, in terms of $E_0,q$ and the fixed parameters of Theorem <a href="#thm:type-II" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:type-II">8</a>, so that the operator with the good-state projections satisfies $$\label{mem:moment-bound}
 \frac{1}{UV}\sum_{\substack{P\in\Omega\\P\ \mathrm{primitive}}}
 \langle \mathbf u_P,(\mathcal A\mathcal A^*)^R\mathbf u_P\rangle
 \le L^{-E_0N}.$$ The choices of $A_0,K$ do not depend on the particular fixed band exponents $a_1<\cdots<a_K$; the threshold for $x$ may depend on them. Consequently, for any prescribed fixed $A>0$, the determinant indicator in the expanded square of the preceding section can be replaced by <a href="#eq:det-major" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:det-major">[eq:det-major]</a>, with error $O_A(XY L^{-A})$. In the resulting major term the disjointness restriction on the two unshared products $a,b$ can be omitted. Equivalently, with the notation of <a href="#det:cauchy-square" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="det:cauchy-square">[det:cauchy-square]</a> and <a href="#maj:sum" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="maj:sum">[maj:sum]</a>, $$\label{mem:replacement-interface}
 \abs{\mathcal Q_Y-\mathcal Q_Y^{\mathrm{maj}}}
      \ll_A XY L^{-A}.$$ The precision needed in the pairing estimate <a href="#eq:det-pairing" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:det-pairing">[eq:det-pairing]</a> for this consequence is independent of $K$.

For every fixed $D>0$, the unrestricted major sum $$\label{maj:sum}
 \begin{split}
 \mathcal Q_Y^{\mathrm{maj}}={}&
 \left(\prod_{i=1}^K V_i^{-2}\right)
 \sum_{a,b,m,n,r,s}
 \eta(a/Y)\eta(b/Y)\alpha_m\overline{\alpha_r}
       \beta_n\overline{\beta_s}\,
 \mathcal H_{\mathfrak M}(bmn-ars;a,b)
 \end{split}$$ satisfies $$\label{maj:bound}
 |\mathcal Q_Y^{\mathrm{maj}}|\ll_D XYL^{-D}.$$ Here $a,b$ run independently over products of one prime from each $\mathcal P_i$, with no disjointness condition, and $\mathcal H_{\mathfrak M}$ denotes the kernel in <a href="#eq:det-major" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:det-major">[eq:det-major]</a>. The estimate is uniform in the coefficient intervals, in $|v|\le L^C$, and in the admissible scale $Y$.

Fix $A_0,B,A>0$. For every character $\chi$ of modulus at most $L^{A_0}$, put $$\label{maj:M-definition}
 M_\alpha(t)=\frac1{H_m}\sum_m\alpha_m\chi(m)m^{it}.$$ Then, uniformly for $|t|\le 2XL^B$, $$\label{maj:M-uniform}
 |M_\alpha(t)|\ll_A L^{-A}.$$ The assertion also holds with conjugated coefficients and characters.

Assume $\sum_\nu\sup|K_\nu|\leq L^{C_1}$, where $m_0,C_1$ are fixed independently of $J$. For every fixed $E>0$ there is $E_{\rm id}>0$, depending only on $E$ and the fixed group data, such that $$\sup_{\Theta\in\mathbb R}
    \|\mathcal T_\zeta(\Theta)\|_{2\to2}\leq L^{-E_{\rm id}}$$ has the following consequence for all sufficiently large fixed $J$. Let $I=[X,2X)$, with $x^\gamma\leq X\leq x^{1/\gamma}$ for fixed $\gamma>0$. If $f$ is supported on positions in $I$, is independent of the chosen marks, and satisfies $$\sup|f|\leq\exp(O(\sqrt L)),\qquad
 \|f\|,\|g\|\leq X^{1/2}L^{C_3},$$ then $$\label{cor:transferred-pairing}
 |\langle f,\mathcal A_\zeta g\rangle|
       \ll XL^{-E+2C_3}.$$ The lower bound on $J$ may also depend on $m_0,C_1$. The threshold for $x$ may depend on $J,C_2,\gamma$. No further restriction on $\zeta$ is imposed beyond the ideal norm hypothesis. Taking absolute values of the individual transition coefficients gives row and column sums at most $L^{C_{\rm abs}}$, where $C_{\rm abs}$ is independent of $J$.

For every $E_{\rm id}>0$ and fixed $C_4\geq0$, there are fixed $m_0,J_0,C_1$ such that, for each fixed $J\geq J_0$, a family consisting of the raw pattern $C_g=\varnothing$, $K_0=1$, and signed comparison patterns satisfies $$\label{cor:ideal-bound}
 \sum_\nu\sup|K_\nu|\leq L^{C_1},\qquad
 \sup_{\substack{\Theta\in\mathbb R\\|\zeta|\leq L^{C_4}}}
       \|\mathcal T_\zeta(\Theta)\|_{2\to2}\leq L^{-E_{\rm id}}.$$ The family is independent of $\Theta,\zeta$; its defining parameters are independent of $J$.*



*Split the big groups into two blocks. Every comparison frees nonempty sets of probes $A,B$ in the respective blocks, with coefficient $$\label{cor:comparison-kernel}
 -(-1)^{|A|+|B|}\mathcal K_A(D_A,X_A)\mathcal K_B(D_B,X_B).$$ Here $D_A,D_B$ are the free products, $X_A$ is the corresponding target-mark product, and $X_B$ the corresponding source-mark product. No shared or final $\ell$ label enters these kernels. For a nonempty slot set $\Lambda$ with at most $m_0$ slots in each group, $$\label{cor:cell-kernel}
 \mathcal K_\Lambda(D,X)
 =\sum_{j:\nu_{\Lambda j}\geq\xi}
   \frac{\mathbf{1}_{\log D\in I_j}\mathbf{1}_{\log X\in I_j}}{\nu_{\Lambda j}}
   \sum_{\substack{\chi\text{ primitive}\\\mathop{\mathrm{cond}}(\chi)\leq Q}}
        \overline{\chi(D)}\chi(X),
 \qquad I_j=[jh,(j+1)h).$$ The number $\nu_{\Lambda j}$ is the cell probability for the independent slot laws. The parameters $Q,h^{-1},\xi^{-1}$ are fixed powers of $L$, with $h\leq1$. There are at most $Q^2$ characters and $O_{m_0}(1+L^{d_0}\log L/h)$ nonempty cells, and $$\sup|\mathcal K_\Lambda|\leq Q^2\xi^{-1},\qquad
 \sup_X\mathbb E_D|\mathcal K_\Lambda(D,X)|\leq Q^2.$$ More precisely, for any prescribed fixed $A_0>0$, the parameters may be chosen, independently of $J$, so that for arbitrary $L^2$ tuple tests $f(X_B),g(X_A)$ and all $\theta\in\mathbb R$, $|\zeta|\leq L^{C_4}$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \big|\mathbb E\,\overline{f(X_B)}g(X_A)
 \big[&(X_AX_B)^{i\zeta}\mathrm{e}(\theta X_AX_B)\notag\\
 &-\mathcal K_A(D_A,X_A)\mathcal K_B(D_B,X_B)
       (D_AD_B)^{i\zeta}\mathrm{e}(\theta D_AD_B)\big]\big|
 \leq L^{-A_0}\|f\|_2\|g\|_2.
 \label{cor:comparison-contract}
\end{aligned}$$ The four label tuples in this expectation are independent; the tests need not depend only on their products. Expanding the two kernels, including all patterns, has total coefficient mass and number of terms bounded by fixed powers of $L$.

Let $a_w$ be supported on $w\asymp Y$, where $Y=x^{1+o(1)}$, and suppose $H=x^{o(1)}\to\infty$. For every fixed integer $j\geq1$, $$\label{cor:local-mellin}
 \int_{|\beta|\leq H^{-1}}|\widehat a(\beta)|^2\,\,\mathrm d\beta
 \ll_j\frac1Y\int_{\mathbb R}(1+H|t|/Y)^{-j}
       \left|\sum_w a_ww^{it}\right|^2\,\,\mathrm dt
       +\left(\frac HY\right)^2\sum_w|a_w|^2.$$

Let $H_{\rm marks}(n)=\mathcal W(n)W_{\mathbf t}^{\mathbf b}(n)$, where $0\leq t_g\leq m'$ for fixed $m'$ and $|\mathbf b|\leq1$. In particular, zero values of $t_g$ are allowed; these occur when a small mark is removed below. There is a divisor sum $$\label{cor:divisor-model}
 H_0(n)=\sum_{d'\mid n}A_{d'},\qquad
 d'\leq\exp(O(L^{.47})),\qquad
 \sum_{d'}\frac{|A_{d'}|}{d'}\leq L^{C_8},$$ whose prime divisors all belong to the active or inert groups, such that, on every positive integer dyad $n\asymp R$, $$\label{cor:model-error}
 \sum_{n\asymp R}|H_{\rm marks}(n)-H_0(n)|^2
       \ll_N R L^{-N}$$ for every fixed $N$. The assertion is uniform in the bounded tuple test $\mathbf b$, with no regularity assumption on that test.

For every nonnegative $\Phi\in C_c^\infty((0,\infty))$, $$\label{eq:interior}
 \sum_{p\text{ prime}}\Phi((p-1)/x)
                   \bigl(f_x(p-1)-C_x\bigr)=o(x/\log x).$$ The limit holds through all real $x\to\infty$ for the slot intervals just described.

Choose $0<c_0<\varepsilon/2$. Let $H$ be a weight from a fixed finite collection of disjoint arrays, a product of two such weights (allowing a repeated array), a constant, or a bounded linear combination of these functions. Then, for every fixed $A>0$, $$\begin{aligned}
\label{ext:progression}
 \sum_{r\leq x^{c_0}}\left|
  \sum_{\substack{u\geq1\\r\mid u+1}}
             \Phi(u/x)f_x(u)H(u)
  -\frac{x}{r}\left(\int_0^\infty\Phi(t)\,\,\mathrm dt\right)
                     C_x M_x(H)\right|
  &\ll_A xL^{-A}.
\end{aligned}$$ The same assertion holds with $f_x,C_x$ replaced by $1,1$. The constants may depend on the fixed arrays and on the bounded coefficients in the linear combination.

There are absolute constants $C_1,C_2>0$ such that, for every integer $k\ge2$, some integer $s$ with $k\le s\le C_1k^4$ satisfies $$\label{lp:mean-value-bound}
 J_{s,k}(M)\le \exp(C_1k^{C_2})M^{2s-K+1/100}
 \qquad(M\ge1).$$

Fix $C>0$, let $q\le L^C$, and put $s_0=1+r_*+iv$, where $1\le|v|\le\tfrac52x^3$. There is a radius $R$ with $4r_*\le R\le5r_*$, with no zero on its boundary, for which $$\label{lp:local-log-derivative-formula}
 \frac{D'}{D}(s,\chi)
 =\sum_{|\rho-s_0|<R}\frac1{s-\rho}
       +O_C(T^2/r_*)\qquad(|s-s_0|\le2r_*),$$ away from zeros. The sum counts zeros with multiplicity and has $O_C(T^2)$ terms.

For each fixed $C>0$ there is $c=c(C)>0$ such that every character of modulus at most $L^C$ has no zero in $$\label{lp:zero-free-region}
 \operatorname{Re}s\ge1-cT^2/L,\qquad
                    1\le|\operatorname{Im}s|\le x^3.$$ Moreover, $$\label{lp:log-derivative-bound}
 \left|\frac{D'}D(s,\chi)\right|\ll_C L
 \quad\left(1-\frac{cT^2}{2L}\le\operatorname{Re}s\le1+\frac1L,
       \quad2\le|\operatorname{Im}s|\le\frac{x^3}{2}\right).$$
